\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b\fs36 Tools for Analysing Neural Activity in a 24-Week Fetus\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f2 Several noninvasive and minimally invasive tools are available or under research for assessing neural activity in a fetus at 24 weeks of gestation:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. Fetal Magnetoencephalography (fMEG)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fMEG, exemplified by devices like the SARA (SQUID Array for Reproductive Assessment) system, is a noninvasive technique that records the magnetic fields generated by electrical activity in the fetal brain. It offers high temporal resolution and is uniquely suited for detecting real-time neural activity without distortion from maternal tissues{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The SARA system uses an array of sensors shaped to fit the maternal abdomen, allowing for the detection of spontaneous brain activity and evoked responses (such as auditory and visual stimuli) in fetuses as early as 28 weeks, and potentially earlier{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 2. Fetal Electroencephalography (fEEG)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fEEG involves placing electrodes on the fetal scalp (usually during labor after membrane rupture) to record electrical brain activity. Real-time spectral analysis of fEEG can detect various brain activity patterns, including sleep cycles and responses to hypoxia{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/pr2000199"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While most studies focus on intrapartum monitoring, fEEG has been used in both mature and premature fetuses, and automated methods for EEG analysis can help identify abnormal patterns{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/pr2000199"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 3. Functional Magnetic Resonance Imaging (fMRI)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fMRI provides both functional and anatomical information and can be used to assess fetal brain activity and connectivity. However, it has limitations regarding temporal resolution and safety concerns related to prolonged exposure{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fMRI is more commonly used for structural and connectivity studies rather than real-time neural activity monitoring in fetuses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 4. Ultrasound-Based Neurosonography
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Advanced neurosonography (high-resolution ultrasound) is routinely used to assess fetal brain development and detect structural abnormalities during the second trimester (18\'9624 weeks){\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://academic.oup.com/brain/article/147/12/3982/7721052"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While ultrasound does not directly measure neural activity, it can identify malformations or developmental deviations that may be associated with abnormal brain function{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://academic.oup.com/brain/article/147/12/3982/7721052"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf0 \strokec2 <$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b\fs36 Detection of Increased or Abnormal Neural Activity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f1\b Detection Capabilities:
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fMEG and fEEG
\f2\b0  can detect increased or abnormal neural activity, such as atypical patterns, excessive synchrony, or altered responses to stimuli. For example, spectral analysis of fEEG can reveal changes in frequency and amplitude associated with fetal distress, hypoxia, or abnormal sleep cycles{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/pr2000199"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}.\
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 fMEG
\f2\b0  can monitor spontaneous and evoked brain activity, allowing researchers to identify deviations from typical developmental patterns{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 Clinical Relevance:
\f2\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Abnormal neural activity patterns detected by these methods may indicate risk for brain injury, hypoxia, or developmental disorders. Early detection allows for closer monitoring and potential intervention{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881832/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/pr2000199"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Structural imaging (ultrasound, MRI) complements these findings by identifying anatomical correlates of functional abnormalities{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://academic.oup.com/brain/article/147/12/3982/7721052"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

